 ✅ Q1 :  A service works by IP address but fails by hostname. How do you investigate?
>>  1. If a service is reachable using its IP address but not by its hostname, the problem is usually related to DNS resolution, name lookup configuration
>> 2. Debug in below area
    >> Step 1: Verify DNS Resolution
       >> nslookup hostname
       >> Check Hostname resolves to IP address properly
    >> Step 2: Test Local Name Resolution
       >> Check whether the hostname exists in local configuration.
       >> cat /etc/hosts
    >> Step 3: Check DNS Server Configuration
       >> cat /etc/resolv.conf
    >> Step 4: Compare IP vs Hostname Connectivity
       >> If the service works through IP:curl http://10.10.10.5
       >> but fails with hostname : curl -v http://myserver.example.com
    >> Step 5 : Verify TLS/HTTPS Certificates : For HTTPS services, hostname must match the certificate.
       >> openssl s_client -connect hostname:443
    >> Step 6 : Flush DNS Cache : A stale DNS cache may be causing issues.
    >> Step 7 : Capture Network Traffic , Verify DNS queries and responses.
        tcpdump -ni any port 53

 ✅ Q2 : A certificate renewal causes failures for only some clients. What is your test plan?
   >> Example incident : We renewed a public-facing TLS certificate on an API gateway. Modern browsers continued to work, but several Java 8 applications started failing immediately after deployment. 
Investigation showed the new certificate was issued from a different intermediate CA. The server was sending only the leaf certificate and not the required intermediate certificate.
Browsers succeeded because they fetched or cached the intermediate automatically, while Java clients could not build the trust chain.
Using openssl s_client -showcerts, we confirmed the missing intermediate. 
We restored the correct certificate chain, reloaded the service, 
and failures dropped from 100% to 0% within minutes. We then added certificate-chain validation as part of the certificate renewal process to prevent recurrence.

 ✅  Debug the issue in below formatted steps
     >> Step1 : 1. Validate System Time , Check both server and client clocks.
        >> date
        >> timedatectl status
    >> Step2 : Inspect new certificate time period [ Not Before Not After ]
    >> Step3 : Verify Complete Certificate Chain
       >> One of the most common causes of partial client failures is a missing intermediate CA certificate.
       >> openssl s_client -connect server:443 -showcerts
       >> Questions:
          Is the intermediate being sent?
          Did the intermediate change after renewal?
          Are older clients missing the new intermediate?

  >> Step4 : New certs might generated with new CA, Compare Trust Stores, Some clients may not trust the new issuing CA.
  >> Step 5 : Verify TLS Compatibility,Test various TLS versions. between TLS 1.2, TLS 1.3
  >> Summary : My test plan would include validating certificate attributes (SAN, EKU, validity period, issuer),
verifying the complete certificate chain, checking client trust stores, comparing TLS library versions, confirming time synchronization, 
testing revocation checking, reproducing failures across representative client types, monitoring TLS handshakes during a staged rollout, 
and having clear rollback criteria with the previous certificate and chain readily available. 
This approach quickly isolates whether the issue is due to the certificate itself, trust-chain problems, client compatibility, or environmental factors.





